docs/build-https.md-50-
docs/build-https.md-51-This makes a scope an operator choice over a known repository identity, not a
docs/build-https.md-52-rule that package data can widen. See `Spec §6.1` and `Spec §6.3`.
docs/build-https.md-53-
docs/build-https.md:54:### Entry grammar and token sources
docs/build-https.md-55-
docs/build-https.md-56-Each entry is an object with only these fields:
docs/build-https.md-57-
docs/build-https.md-58-| Field | Required | Meaning |
docs/build-https.md-59-| --- | --- | --- |
docs/build-https.md:60:| `token` | exactly one of `token` or `token_env` | `git-credentials` reads the operator's existing Git HTTPS credential for the scope host. `keyring` reads the manager-namespaced credential stored by `login`. |
docs/build-https.md:61:| `token_env` | exactly one of `token` or `token_env` | An environment variable name. Its value is captured when the Curator process starts. |
docs/build-https.md:62:| `username` | no | Username sent with the resolved token. It defaults to `token`. |
docs/build-https.md-63-
docs/build-https.md-64-The field is parsed strictly. An invalid scope, an unknown field, a non-object
docs/build-https.md-65-entry, an invalid environment variable name, an empty field value, or an entry
docs/build-https.md-66-that names zero or two sources rejects the configuration. The configuration is
docs/build-https.md-67-not partially applied.
docs/build-https.md-68-
docs/build-https.md:69:## `curator config build-https`
docs/build-https.md-70-
docs/build-https.md-71-The command surface is:
docs/build-https.md-72-
docs/build-https.md-73-```text
docs/build-https.md:74:curator config build-https add <scope> (--git-credentials | --keyring | --token-env NAME) [--username NAME]
docs/build-https.md:75:curator config build-https login <scope> [--username NAME]
docs/build-https.md:76:curator config build-https list
docs/build-https.md:77:curator config build-https remove <scope>
docs/build-https.md-78-```
docs/build-https.md-79-
docs/build-https.md-80-`add` records exactly one source and replaces the complete entry under that
docs/build-https.md:81:scope. It prints the source, never the token:
docs/build-https.md-82-
docs/build-https.md-83-```console
docs/build-https.md:84:$ curator config build-https add git.example.com/portals --token-env PORTALS_TOKEN --username oauth2
docs/build-https.md:85:added build_https scope git.example.com/portals: token_env=PORTALS_TOKEN username=oauth2
docs/build-https.md-86-
docs/build-https.md:87:$ curator config build-https add git.example.com/portals --keyring
docs/build-https.md:88:replaced build_https scope git.example.com/portals: source=keyring
docs/build-https.md-89-```
docs/build-https.md-90-
docs/build-https.md:91:`login` reads one token from a hidden terminal prompt. When either standard
docs/build-https.md-92-input or standard error is not a terminal, it reads one line from standard
docs/build-https.md:93:input instead. It stores the token
docs/build-https.md:94:through the operator's Git credential machinery under a scope-namespaced
docs/build-https.md:95:entry, then records a `keyring` source for that scope. A token is never a
docs/build-https.md-96-command-line argument and is never written as a literal configuration value.
docs/build-https.md-97-
docs/build-https.md-98-`list` sorts scopes and reports whether their named source resolves now:
docs/build-https.md-99-
docs/build-https.md-100-```console
docs/build-https.md:101:$ curator config build-https list
docs/build-https.md-102-git.example.com/portals	source=keyring present=true
docs/build-https.md:103:git.example.com/tools	token_env=TOOLS_TOKEN username=oauth2 present=false
docs/build-https.md-104-```
docs/build-https.md-105-
docs/build-https.md-106-With no configured scopes, `list` exits zero, prints no standard output, and
docs/build-https.md:107:reports `curator: no build_https scopes are configured` on standard error.
docs/build-https.md-108-`remove` removes the selection. For a `keyring` selection it also removes the
docs/build-https.md:109:manager-namespaced stored token; it never removes the operator's own Git
docs/build-https.md:110:credential selected by `git-credentials`.
docs/build-https.md-111-
docs/build-https.md-112-## Resolution, precheck, and candidates
docs/build-https.md-113-
docs/build-https.md:114:Before the first external repository fetch, Curator resolves HTTPS selections
docs/build-https.md:115:for every HTTPS build repository in the planned closure. This gives a selected
docs/build-https.md-116-but unavailable source a deterministic failure before any repository fetch:
docs/build-https.md-117-
docs/build-https.md:118:- `git-credentials` requires an existing operator Git credential for the
docs/build-https.md-119-  scope host.
docs/build-https.md:120:- `keyring` requires a token previously stored by `login` for that scope.
docs/build-https.md:121:- `token_env` requires the named variable to have been non-empty at process
docs/build-https.md-122-  entry.
docs/build-https.md-123-
docs/build-https.md:124:No configured or environment source is not an error. HTTPS has an anonymous
docs/build-https.md-125-transport, so an uncovered repository can be fetched anonymously. On an
docs/build-https.md-126-operator terminal, however, an uncovered private repository first opens a
docs/build-https.md:127:candidate prompt before any fetch. The prompt offers an existing Git HTTPS
docs/build-https.md:128:credential for that host when presence-only discovery finds one, or lets the
docs/build-https.md:129:operator enter a token now. No candidate is read or used until the operator
docs/build-https.md-130-selects it. The operator then chooses a scope to persist or a this-run-only
docs/build-https.md:131:(`r`) choice; the latter never writes configuration or credential storage.
docs/build-https.md-132-Aborting the prompt stops the run rather than silently falling back to
docs/build-https.md:133:anonymous HTTPS.
docs/build-https.md-134-
docs/build-https.md:135:Headless, non-terminal, and dry-run runs never prompt. Their uncovered HTTPS
docs/build-https.md-136-repositories continue anonymously. `list` is a presence-only check for
docs/build-https.md:137:configured candidates; it does not reveal tokens or select a credential for a
docs/build-https.md-138-repository.
docs/build-https.md-139-
docs/build-https.md:140:For an HTTPS address in a manifest, include the `.git` suffix. The service may
docs/build-https.md-141-otherwise answer with a `301` redirect, and Curator's protected fetch rejects
docs/build-https.md:142:redirects rather than forwarding a request or credential.
docs/build-https.md-143-
docs/build-https.md-144-## Precedence and exposure warning
docs/build-https.md-145-
docs/build-https.md-146-Resolution order is:
docs/build-https.md-147-
docs/build-https.md:148:1. `CURATOR_BUILD_HTTPS_TOKEN`, optionally restricted by
docs/build-https.md:149:   `CURATOR_BUILD_HTTPS_HOST` to one exact canonical host.
docs/build-https.md:150:2. The longest matching `build_https` scope.
docs/build-https.md:151:3. On an operator terminal, the credential candidate prompt for an uncovered
docs/build-https.md-152-   repository.
docs/build-https.md:153:4. Anonymous HTTPS when neither applies and no terminal prompt is active.
docs/build-https.md-154-
docs/build-https.md:155:When `CURATOR_BUILD_HTTPS_HOST` is set, repositories on other hosts resolve as
docs/build-https.md-156-though the run-wide override were absent. They may use a matching configured
docs/build-https.md-157-scope, be offered candidates on an operator terminal, or remain anonymous.
docs/build-https.md-158-
docs/build-https.md:159:> Warning: `CURATOR_BUILD_HTTPS_TOKEN` without
docs/build-https.md:160:> `CURATOR_BUILD_HTTPS_HOST` is identity-unbound. It is offered to every
docs/build-https.md:161:> private HTTPS build repository host the run reaches. Set
docs/build-https.md:162:> `CURATOR_BUILD_HTTPS_HOST` to bind the override to one host, or use a
docs/build-https.md:163:> `build_https` scope, unless every reached host is intended to receive that
docs/build-https.md:164:> token. This disclosure is required by `Spec core §12.2`.
docs/build-https.md-165-
docs/build-https.md-166-## Platform and fetch mechanism
docs/build-https.md-167-
docs/build-https.md:168:Curator uses the operator's `git credential` interface to read, store, and
docs/build-https.md:169:delete credentials. That mechanism uses the credential helper configured by
docs/build-https.md-170-the operator's Git installation, so the same configuration surface works on
docs/build-https.md-171-macOS, Linux, and Windows. Curator does not ask a fetch process to discover
docs/build-https.md:172:credentials, and it disables Git terminal prompting during the protected
docs/build-https.md-173-fetch.
docs/build-https.md-174-
docs/build-https.md-175-For an authenticated fetch, Curator materializes a private copy of its fixed
docs/build-https.md-176-askpass broker. The broker receives only the exact username and password
docs/build-https.md-177-prompts for the selected host. Its state file contains the host and username;
docs/build-https.md-178-the secret exists only in that fetch process tree. Other Git subprocesses do
docs/build-https.md-179-not receive the broker state or secret. The broker is bound to the resolved
docs/build-https.md:180:host, so a credential selected for one host cannot answer a prompt for another.
--
docs/build-ssh.md:1:# Operator SSH credentials for external build repositories
docs/build-ssh.md-2-
docs/build-ssh.md-3-A schema-7 skill may declare an external build repository: a Git repository
docs/build-ssh.md-4-Curator fetches and builds a command from. When that repository is reached over
docs/build-ssh.md-5-SSH, something has to decide which key authenticates the fetch. That decision is
docs/build-ssh.md:6:operator-owned. Git and SSH executables, credentials, host-verification state,
docs/build-ssh.md-7-proxy policy, timeouts, and authentication mode `MUST NOT` be selected by a
docs/build-ssh.md-8-manifest, descriptor, repository, substitution, compiler environment, receipt
docs/build-ssh.md-9-trust field, or marker (`Spec §12.2`). A package can never choose the key that
docs/build-ssh.md-10-reaches its own repository.
--
docs/build-ssh.md-12-Curator implements that rule as a fail-closed selection surface. There is no
docs/build-ssh.md-13-fallback onto ambient SSH state: a fetch that succeeds because some unrelated
docs/build-ssh.md-14-key happened to be loaded in the operator agent is a fetch nobody authorized.
docs/build-ssh.md-15-An SSH build repository the operator has not covered stops the run with
docs/build-ssh.md:16:`build_repository_ssh_credential_missing` before the first fetch.
